#: ../../../../../Wrapping/Python/vtkmodules/util/dlpack_support.py:22
msgid "Lifetime"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../../../Wrapping/Python/vtkmodules/util/dlpack_support.py:24
msgid ""
"That only works if the memory outlives whoever is looking at it, which is "
"the part these protocols exist to get right."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../../../Wrapping/Python/vtkmodules/util/dlpack_support.py:27
msgid ""
"Exporting builds the capsule around a ``vtkMemoryDescriptor``, which holds a "
"reference to the source array; the reference is dropped when the consumer "
"releases the tensor. So a VTK array stays alive for exactly as long as a "
"CuPy view of it does, even if nothing else refers to it."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../../../Wrapping/Python/vtkmodules/util/dlpack_support.py:32
msgid ""
"Importing goes the other way: the imported array holds the DLPack capsule "
"open, so the CuPy array behind it cannot be collected while VTK is using it."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../../../Wrapping/Python/vtkmodules/util/dlpack_support.py:36
msgid ""
"Neither direction requires the caller to think about this, which is the "
"point -- an interop layer that needs a lifetime rule written in the docs is "
"one that will be got wrong."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../../../Wrapping/Python/vtkmodules/util/dlpack_support.py:40
msgid "Device support"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../../../Wrapping/Python/vtkmodules/util/dlpack_support.py:42
msgid ""
"Host arrays work with plain VTK. In VTK built with Viskores, device memory "
"of a kind Viskores was built for is held by Viskores' arrays "
"(``vtkmDataArrayFactory``), so Viskores' filters run on it. Any other device "
"memory is held by VTK's own ``vtkDeviceDataArray`` wherever the vendor's "
"driver or runtime loads -- VTK needs no GPU toolkit for it, only the driver "
"at run time (CUDA, HIP and Level Zero). ``VTK_DLPACK_DEVICE_ARRAYS=vtk`` in "
"the environment prefers VTK's own arrays where both could. Without either, "
"importing a device tensor raises ``RuntimeError`` rather than silently "
"copying to the host."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../../../Wrapping/Python/vtkmodules/util/dlpack_support.py:52
msgid ""
"Metal tensors are rejected. The data pointer a Metal tensor carries is the "
"``MTLBuffer`` object itself, not memory the host can read -- PyTorch's MPS "
"tensors, for one, are private-storage buffers with no host-readable contents "
"at all -- and Viskores has no Metal device adapter to hand it to instead. "
"Copy the tensor to the CPU first. VTK never *exports* Metal memory either, "
"because no VTK array can hold it."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../../../Wrapping/Python/vtkmodules/util/dlpack_support.py:59
msgid "Context, device and streams"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../../../Wrapping/Python/vtkmodules/util/dlpack_support.py:61
msgid ""
"Three things this module does not carry, all of them worth knowing before "
"relying on it for device memory."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../../../Wrapping/Python/vtkmodules/util/dlpack_support.py:64
msgid ""
"**Context.** DLPack identifies a device with two integers, a type and an "
"ordinal; there is nowhere in the protocol to put a context handle. CUDA and "
"HIP do not need one -- the runtime keeps an implicitly shared primary "
"context per device, so a pointer identifies itself and every consumer lands "
"in the same place. Level Zero does not work that way: a USM pointer is only "
"meaningful inside the ``ze_context_handle_t`` it was allocated from, and "
"recovering that requires a context you already hold."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../../../Wrapping/Python/vtkmodules/util/dlpack_support.py:72
msgid ""
"So for ``kDLOneAPI`` the context travels once, out of band, instead of with "
"every tensor. VTK has one Level Zero context per process, and "
"``level_zero_handles()`` reports its driver, device and context; a producer "
"that wants VTK to read its memory allocates *there*. The context is, in "
"order: one a producer gave VTK "
"(``vtkDeviceArrayFactory.SetLevelZeroContext(driver, device, context)``, for "
"a library whose context VTK cannot make, such as a SYCL runtime's default "
"context), else the one Viskores' SYCL queue runs in where VTK is built with "
"Viskores on Kokkos' SYCL backend, else one VTK makes on the first Level Zero "
"GPU the first time it is asked, and keeps. Give VTK a context before "
"importing any Level Zero tensor. On import the pointer is checked against "
"that context and refused if Level Zero does not know it as device or shared "
"memory. The check separates device memory from host memory; it cannot prove "
"which context a pointer came from, because drivers are not required to tell "
"contexts apart and Intel's does not. Exported Level Zero tensors are in that "
"context too. Without the Level Zero loader and a GPU (and without Viskores) "
"``kDLOneAPI`` tensors are refused, naming the space."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../../../Wrapping/Python/vtkmodules/util/dlpack_support.py:90
msgid ""
"**Device ordinal.** An exported tensor's ``device_id`` is the number of the "
"device its memory is on, as the vendor's runtime counts devices: CUDA's and "
"HIP's device ordinal, or the device's index among its Level Zero driver's "
"devices (``vtkDeviceArrayFactory.GetDeviceOrdinal``). Where the vendor's "
"library cannot tell, it is 0. ``vtk_to_dlpack(array, device_id=...)`` and "
"``DLPackArray(array, device_id=...)`` override it."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../../../Wrapping/Python/vtkmodules/util/dlpack_support.py:97
msgid ""
"**Streams.** ``__dlpack__`` accepts DLPack's ``stream`` argument and ignores "
"it. That argument is how a consumer says \"make this safe to read on my "
"stream\", so a producer with asynchronous work still queued -- Viskores on a "
"Kokkos backend, for instance -- can hand over memory whose contents are not "
"there yet, and the race has no diagnostic. Synchronize before exporting if "
"anything asynchronous produced the data."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../../../Wrapping/Python/vtkmodules/util/dlpack_support.py:104
msgid ""
"The same holds for memory VTK imports and reads on the host through "
"``vtkDeviceDataArray``. For CUDA and HIP, VTK synchronizes the memory's "
"device before copying, so work the producer queued on any stream is done. "
"Level Zero has no device-wide synchronize, and VTK copies on a command list "
"of its own, so it does not wait for kernels a producer still has queued (on "
"a SYCL queue, for instance): synchronize the producer before VTK reads a "
"``kDLOneAPI`` tensor."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../../../Wrapping/Python/vtkmodules/util/dlpack_support.py:7
msgid "Zero-copy exchange between VTK arrays and DLPack-speaking libraries."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../../../Wrapping/Python/vtkmodules/util/dlpack_support.py:9
msgid ""
"DLPack is the interchange protocol used by CuPy, PyTorch, JAX, NumPy and "
"TensorFlow. Speaking it means a VTK array can be handed to any of them -- "
"and taken back -- without copying, on the host or on a GPU."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../../../Wrapping/Python/vtkmodules/util/dlpack_support.py:13
msgid ""
"from vtkmodules.util.dlpack_support import vtk_to_dlpack, dlpack_to_vtk\n"
"\n"
"import cupy\n"
"gpu = cupy.from_dlpack(vtk_to_dlpack(vtk_array))   # VTK -> CuPy\n"
"back = dlpack_to_vtk(gpu)                          # CuPy -> VTK\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../../../Wrapping/Python/vtkmodules/util/dlpack_support.py:19
msgid ""
"Both directions are views. Nothing is copied, so both sides are looking at "
"the same memory, and a write through one is visible through the other."
msgstr ""

